<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">New York</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> is a major city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the home of the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. A reproduction of the treaty between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, often cited as one of the earliest known peace treaties, hangs at the United Nations headquarters there.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Overseas, the VOC’s posts at Batavia, in Ceylon and at the Cape and the West India Company’s colonies in New Netherland, Suriname and the Caribbean left lasting marks, including <b>New York</b>, founded as New Amsterdam, and the Afrikaans-speaking population of South Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Repeated wars in the seventeenth century devastated the land, and in the eighteenth century poverty and religious pressure drove thousands of Palatines to emigrate, many to Pennsylvania and <b>New York</b>, where they formed a large part of the Pennsylvania Dutch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A replica of the Hittite-Egyptian peace treaty, the earliest to survive in both parties' versions, hangs at the United Nations headquarters in <b>New York</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Haudenosaunee, the People of the Longhouse, were Iroquoian-speaking farming peoples of what is now upstate <b>New York</b>, living in palisaded villages of bark longhouses between the Hudson River and the Genesee.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…1609 the English captain Henry Hudson, hired by the Dutch East India Company to find a passage to Asia, sailed the flyboat Halve Maen into Upper <b>New York</b> Bay and up the river now named for him until the water grew too shallow at the site of Troy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of Panama’s significance is inseparable from the Panama Canal, opened on 15 August 1914, which shortened the sea voyage between <b>New York</b> and San Francisco by some 13,000 kilometres and transformed world shipping, naval strategy and the trade of the Pacific coast of the Americas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
